latest_plan = 'Based on our reflection, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Input Grid:\n   a. Identify all color regions (excluding gray and black).\n   b. Calculate the size and bounding box for each region.\n   c. Determine the color hierarchy based on total area of each color.\n\n2. Identify the Main Structure:\n   a. Select the largest color region as the main structure.\n   b. Simplify this region into a rectangular shape, preserving any significant "holes" or internal structures.\n\n3. Process Secondary Regions:\n   a. Sort remaining regions by size, from largest to smallest.\n   b. For each secondary region:\n      - If it\'s large enough, consider it as a separate structure.\n      - If it\'s small, determine if it should be integrated into the main structure or placed adjacent.\n\n4. Create the Output Grid:\n   a. Initialize an empty grid of the same size as the input.\n   b. Place the main structure on the grid.\n   c. For each secondary region:\n      - If it\'s a separate structure, place it adjacent to the main structure, maintaining relative position if possible.\n      - If it\'s to be integrated, find an appropriate location within or adjacent to the main structure.\n\n5. Optimize Layout:\n   a. Adjust the positions of regions to minimize empty space.\n   b. Ensure all regions are rectangular.\n   c. Maintain the general spatial relationships between regions.\n\n6. Final Adjustments:\n   a. Remove any remaining gray dots.\n   b. Add a black border around the entire structure if necessary.\n   c. Trim any unnecessary empty rows or columns from the edges of the grid.\n\n7. Verify and Refine:\n   a. Check that all colors from the input (except gray) are present in the output.\n   b. Ensure that the relative sizes of color regions are roughly maintained.\n   c. Verify that the output is more structured and compact than the input.\n\n8. Handle Special Cases:\n   a. Implement specific rules for colors that consistently appear in certain ways (e.g., sky blue often appears in the center).\n   b. Develop a method to preserve significant features of the main structure (like "holes").\n\n9. Implement Flexibility:\n   a. Create parameters that can be adjusted based on the specific input (e.g., threshold for what\'s considered a "small" region).\n   b. Develop multiple strategies for integrating smaller regions and select the best one based on the input characteristics.\n\n10. Testing and Refinement:\n    a. Test the algorithm against all provided examples and additional test cases.\n    b. Refine the algorithm based on test results, focusing on areas where it doesn\'t perform as expected.\n    c. Iterate on the design, potentially returning to earlier steps if major changes are needed.\n\nThis plan provides a structured approach to solving the challenge while maintaining the flexibility needed to handle various input scenarios. The key is to balance the preservation of important features from the input with the goal of creating a more structured and compact output.'
